units.brief:
Common rules. Repository /home/pnathan/src/electric-sunflowers, branch trunk. Read CLAUDE.md, README.md, docs/engine-design.md and the sections of docs/features-2.md named below first. Edit only the files you own; if you need a change elsewhere, stop and report it. Never run git switch, checkout, stash, commit, reset, rebase or push: the orchestrator commits and pushes after it verifies the wave. Other units work in the same tree at the same time; if the workspace does not build because of another unit's half-done work, test with -p <your crate> and say so. Plain ASCII in code, comments and docs; never use the four banned words listed in CLAUDE.md (Owner and conventions). Build offline: every crate is vendored in vendor/; add no crate that is not already there. Doc comments name the algorithm and its source. No fabricated placeholder data presented as real. When you change a public signature that a crate you do not own calls, keep the old one as a thin shim. Invariant: a song without the new fields renders exactly as before, so the demo stems stay bit-identical (scripts/gate.sh --strict passes).

Read docs/features-2.md sections 4.7 and 5.1. Read crates/songwriter/src completely (prompt.rs, styles.rs, lib.rs) and crates/songwriter/tests/prompt.rs. The prompt wording is the product's: change it on purpose, in the existing voice (plain, exact).

1. styles.rs: Style gains duet: DuetFit { Welcome, Occasional, Rare } and phrasing: song::Phrasing. DuetFit: Welcome for bakersfield, nashville, texas, cajun, zydeco, americana, laurel, revival, gospel; Rare for appalachian, broadside, irishair, scottish, welsh, blues; Occasional for the rest. Phrasing delivery: legato for irishair, scottish, welsh, gospel; parlando for blues, broadside, texas, cowboy; detached for oldtime, bluegrass, cajun, zydeco, shanty, irishpub; flowing otherwise. Endings: held for gospel, revival, nashville, irishair; clipped for oldtime, bluegrass, shanty, zydeco; released otherwise. Direction carries both. Style::apply sets song.phrasing = Some(style.phrasing) when it is None (no repair: it is the style's default, as the guitar pattern is); a duet's B phrasing stays None unless written (phrasing_of falls back to the song's). Existing draws in style_direction do not change (no new rng draws).
2. lib.rs: WriteOptions { duet: DuetRequest } with DuetRequest { Auto, Solo, Duet { a: Option<Voice>, b: Option<Voice> } } (Default Auto); write_song_with(claude, &WriteRequest, &WriteOptions, rng); write_song stays = write_song_with(.., &WriteOptions::default(), ..). Do not add fields to WriteRequest (other crates build it literally).
3. prompt.rs: song_prompt gains the duet request (keep a shim with today's signature). New block SINGERS after STYLE:
   - solo or duet is the songwriter's choice unless the request says; a duet suits courtship, a quarrel, a dialogue, a story from two sides, a call-and-response work song; not a private confession or a one-voice narrative;
   - "In this style a duet is common | occasional | rare" from DuetFit;
   - the classic duet is a man and a woman: A baritone or tenor, B alto or soprano;
   - how parts trade: verses split (A one verse, B the next, or alternate lines), the chorus together with the melody for whoever the song belongs to and the other in harmony, the bridge as call and response, the last chorus together; octave blend for a unison hook line;
   - with DuetRequest::Solo or Duet the prompt states the choice (and the voices when given) as fixed.
   New block PHRASING: delivery legato (ballads, airs, hymns), flowing (most songs), parlando (talking blues, story and comic songs), detached (dance tunes, shanties with bite); endings held (anthems, final lines), released (most), clipped (patter); "This style usually sings {delivery}, endings {endings}."
   Reply template: add "phrasing":{"delivery":"<legato|flowing|parlando|detached>","endings":"<held|released|clipped>"} after "voice"; one line describing the optional "duet":{"voice":"<...>","phrasing":{...}} and the optional line/section fields "sing":"A|B|both", "lead":"A|B", "blend":"harmony|octave", with an example chorus line.
4. Tests (tests/prompt.rs): the prompt names the style's duet fit and phrasing; Solo and Duet requests appear verbatim as fixed choices; the reply template parses as the schema expects (feed the mock reply of a duet through write_song_with and song::normalize_value with zero repairs); Style::apply fills phrasing only when None; every style has both new fields (table test); the random draws are unchanged (same Direction for seeds 0..100 as before: record the style/meter/form/mode sequence before editing and compare).
